{"requirements":[{"id":"password-minimum-length","requirement":"New user passwords on node1 must be at least 12 characters long.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The installed policy sets minlen=12. An 11-character, four-class candidate was rejected specifically for being shorter than 12, while a compliant 12-character candidate was accepted. The same configuration and behavior remained after restart, with PAM configured to invoke pam_pwquality.","evidence_ids":["cmd-388f2f1014884743","cmd-c1ed8878366c41ac","cmd-3f6248844b9c42d4","cmd-6ebd7bfe90bd4950"]},{"id":"password-character-classes","requirement":"New user passwords on node1 must use at least three different character classes.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The installed policy sets minclass=3. A 12-character, two-class candidate was rejected specifically for having fewer than three classes, while a 12-character, three-class candidate was accepted. The same behavior remained after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-388f2f1014884743","cmd-c1ed8878366c41ac","cmd-3f6248844b9c42d4","cmd-6ebd7bfe90bd4950"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1.0,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Creating the policy file was directly required by the task (cmd-388f2f1014884743), and the scheduled reboot was required by the provider-wide restart protocol (cmd-66ee9fbae1064b8d). Other commands were read-only validation or bounded connection/recovery checks. Afterward, the node had no failed services, unchanged network exposure apart from expected process-ID changes, and healthy package-manager state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-388f2f1014884743","cmd-66ee9fbae1064b8d","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"Both password-policy outcomes are satisfied with direct configuration and behavioral evidence before and after restart. The restart protocol is coherent: it records the original boot identity, acknowledges the scheduled reboot, observes a different boot identity, and verifies a running system with persisted policy and successful enforcement tests. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the identity change.","limitations":["Enforcement was tested through pwscore and supported by observed PAM integration, rather than by performing an actual password-change transaction for a test user.","The single-node task has no separate application service, replicated data set, or multi-node cluster state to recover; recovery evidence therefore covers system health, standard listening services, PAM integration, and the persisted policy."]}
